; adder_model.tpu — the entire `adder_ternary_vanilla` model, one program.
;
; model/transformer.py's whole forward pass for the shipped ternary adder config
; (d=128, f=128, 2 layers, 4 heads, head_dim=32, T=32, vocab=13), in its
; *current* form: no bias, no positional encoding, DyT for normalization, ReLU
; attention, ReLU feed-forward, dropout off. Two transformer layers and the
; output head, in one run:
;
;     for L in 0..1:
;         Q,K,V = Wq(X), Wk(X), Wv(X)          # ternary weights, no bias
;         K,V   = trit(K), trit(V)             # ternary *activations*
;         S     = Q @ K^T / sqrt(head_dim)     # per head, on the MXU
;         P     = relu(S + causal_mask)        # NOT softmax
;         A     = P @ V                        # [T, 4*32] = [T, 128], on the MXU
;         X     = norm1(X + (Wo(A) + X))       # DyT; note the DOUBLE residual
;         X     = norm2(X + W2(relu(W1(X))))   # DyT
;     logits = X @ fc.weight                   # ternary head, on the MXU
;
; Nothing in this program runs on the VPU's matmul any more. `Model.fc` is a
; TernaryLinear too, so the output head is a `matmul_t` like everything else, and
; `vecmatmul` — which was the head's last caller after ternary K and V took
; attention away from it — is now unreachable from this kernel. The VPU is left
; with the elementwise passes and the narrows.
;
; Three things in that loop a reader of the class hierarchy would not predict,
; and that this kernel therefore has to reproduce deliberately:
;
;   * **K and V are ternary, Q and P are int8.** The MXU multiplies an int8
;     activation by a *ternary weight*, so the only way an attention matmul can
;     run on the array is if the operand that lands on the weight side is
;     ternary — K in `Q @ K^T`, V in `P @ V`. Both are, so both matmuls are
;     `matmul_t` dispatches. They used to be `vecmatmul`, which issues one
;     serial int8 dot product per output element and was the slowest thing in
;     the program; the VPU now runs only the elementwise passes and the head.
;     The narrow that produces a trit is `tquant`: `requant`'s fixed point
;     clipped to +-1 and written 2 bits wide in the array's own weight encoding,
;     so no repacking pass sits between the projection and the matmul.
;   * `MultiHeadAttention.forward` ends in `return O + X`, and
;     `Transformer.forward` then adds `X` again — so the attention residual is
;     `2X + O`, not `X + O`. `vecadd` takes two int8 operands, so that is two
;     adds and an extra narrow between them (RQ_XO, pinned to {1,0} because the
;     second add needs both sides back on X's scale).
;   * `norm1`/`norm2` are DyT — `hardtanh(alpha*x, -1, 1)` with one learned
;     scalar, no gamma/beta (arxiv 2503.10622). On this datapath that is not a
;     new pass at all: it is the narrow the residual add already needed, with
;     the multiplier carrying `alpha` and the output scale pinned to 1/127 so
;     the clip *is* the hardtanh. The `dyt` instruction is `requant` with a
;     symmetric +-127 clip, because hardtanh is odd and int8's -128 is not.
;     ../adder_kernel.md §4.
;
; The host supplies the embedded X (there is no positional encoding to add any
; more), both layers' weights, the mask, the requant words and the quantized
; `fc` weight; it gets the int32 logits back, plus every layer's X as a
; checkpoint. Embedding and argmax stay on the host: the ISA has no gather, and
; `redmax` returns the max rather than its index.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Why this is one program
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The two layers' ternary weights are 48 KB packed, and the last of them starts
; at 0xC400 — past the 64 KB line once the checkpoint grows back, and already
; past it when this was a four-layer model. That used to be unreachable:
; `scalar_unit.sv` truncated a rdmem/wrmem DRAM address to ADDR_W = 16 bits, so
; a program could only name the low 64 KB of the 512 KB SRAM chip, even though
; the DMA, the SRAM controller and the UART host all addressed the full 19 bits.
; The scalar unit now emits MEM_ADDR_W, so the whole chip is program-visible and
; the model fits with 432 KB to spare.
;
; Two consequences shape the code below:
;
;   * X never round-trips through DRAM between layers — it lives in the
;     scratchpad and each layer updates it in place. The per-layer `wrmem` near
;     the bottom is a *checkpoint* for verification, not a data path.
;   * Weights are addressed by a running base register `wl`, advanced one layer
;     block per iteration. `li` is a 16-bit immediate and a layer block base
;     leaves it as soon as the model has more than two layers (layer 3's block
;     started at 0x1AC00), so the base is built by `adds` — which is exactly why
;     the globals sit below 0x5000 where they *are* li-able.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The four sharp edges, stated once
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. `cfg vlen` is 10 bits, so the maximum VPU vector length is 1023 — and a
;    [32][32] score block is exactly 1024 elements. `vlen = 1024` reads as 0 and
;    the dispatch silently does nothing. Every elementwise pass here therefore
;    runs in CHUNK = 512-element pieces.
;
; 2. `vecadd` adds two int8 *operands*, so a residual add only means anything if
;    both sides are at the same scale. That is a constraint on the host's
;    requant words, not on this program: RQ_O and RQ_XO must land on X's scale
;    and RQ_F must land W2's output on X1's. ../adder_kernel.md §4.
;
; 3. DyT is what bounds the residual stream, and it is the reason this kernel is
;    worth running at int8 at all. Without normalization nothing renormalizes X
;    between layers, each layer's requant multiplies it by a constant, and the
;    error compounds geometrically — which is the 0.00% exact-sequence result in
;    ../adder_kernel.md §7.6. `norm2` bounds its output to [-1, 1]
;    *analytically*, so every layer after the first enters at a known scale of
;    1/127 with no calibration involved.
;
; 4. `tquant` writes 2 bits per element, i.e. one byte per four, so its `vlen`
;    must be a multiple of 4 and its destination pointer advances at a *quarter*
;    the rate of its source. CHUNK/4 = PKCH = 128 is that step. Both packed
;    tensors are exactly 1024 bytes, which is also why nothing has to be
;    rebuilt per head: a head is a byte offset into the packed block, because
;    the array reads a weight column as a contiguous run of trits.
;
;     python ../assembler.py adder_model.tpu --listing
;     python ../gen_vectors.py -p examples/adder_model.tpu

; ===========================================================================
; Geometry — model/transformer.py adder_ternary_vanilla()
; ===========================================================================
.equ T        32            ; tokens (train.py --max_tokens; <= 63 for cfg tlen)
.equ D        128           ; model dim
.equ HEADS    4             ; q_heads == kv_heads, so heads_per_q = 1
.equ DH       32            ; head_dim = D // HEADS
.equ D3       D * 3         ; fused Q|K|V weight block: 3D columns
.equ F        128           ; feed-forward hidden dim
.equ VOCAB    13            ; len(numbers_data.VOCAB)
.equ LAYERS   2

.equ ROWS     8             ; MXU array contraction dim
.equ COLS     8             ; MXU array output cols

; The array writes whole COLS-wide output tiles, so a 13-column head would have
; its last tile spill three int32 words past the end of each logit row and land
; on the *next* row's first three — n is the outer loop, so it would corrupt
; every row but the last. Rounding the head's output up to a whole tile is the
; fix: 16 columns, of which the host stages the top three as zero trits, and the
; host reads 13 of every 16 back. See section 8.
.equ VPAD     (VOCAB + COLS - 1) // COLS * COLS  ; head output cols, padded = 16

; Tile counts, one pair per matmul shape in the program. The three names that
; contract over D are the projections and the feed-forward; the four short ones
; are attention, where the contraction is a head_dim (scores) or the token axis
; (values). DH and T are both 32 today, so KTDH/NTT/KTT/NTDH all come out at 4 —
; they are still written separately, because a change to either would move only
; two of the four.
.equ KTD      D // ROWS     ; contraction tiles, D-deep matmul       = 16
.equ NTD      D // COLS     ; output tiles, 128 columns              = 16
.equ NTV      VPAD // COLS  ; output tiles, the padded head          =  2
.equ KTDH     DH // ROWS    ; contraction tiles, Q @ K^T             =  4
.equ NTT      T // COLS     ; output tiles, T keys                   =  4
.equ KTT      T // ROWS     ; contraction tiles, P @ V               =  4
.equ NTDH     DH // COLS    ; output tiles, one head's DH columns    =  4

.equ CHUNK    512           ; VPU pass length (cfg vlen is 10 bits: max 1023)
.equ PKCH     CHUNK // 4    ; ...and the bytes one `tquant` pass writes   = 128
.equ NCHUNK   T * D // CHUNK    ; passes over a [T][D] activation = 8

; ---- strides (bytes) ------------------------------------------------------
.equ AROW     D             ; activation row stride: D int8             = 128
.equ WCOL     D * 2 // 8    ; ternary column stride, D trits deep       = 32
.equ TCOL     T * 2 // 8    ; ternary column stride, T trits deep       =  8
.equ KHOFF    DH * 2 // 8   ; head step inside a packed K row           =  8
.equ VHOFF    DH * TCOL     ; head step inside packed V^T             = 256
.equ CROWD    D * 4         ; int32 row stride of a [T][D] out          = 512
.equ SROW     T * 4         ; int32 row stride of a [T][T] block        = 128
.equ LROW     VPAD * 4      ; int32 row stride of the [T][VPAD] logits  = 64

; ===========================================================================
; Scratchpad map (64 KB; highest byte used is 0xCC3F)
; ===========================================================================
.equ WBUF     0x0000        ; weight window, refilled 4x/layer     (12288 B)
.equ Q8       0x3000        ; [T][D] int8 Q                         (4096 B)
.equ K8       0x4000        ; [T][D] int8 K, before ternarizing     (4096 B)
.equ V8       0x5000        ; [T][D] int8 V, before transposing     (4096 B)
.equ VT       0x6000        ; [D][T] int8, V transposed, all heads   (4096 B)
.equ X        0x7000        ; [T][D] int8 residual stream, in place  (4096 B)
.equ T1       0x8000        ; [T][D] int8 temp: A, H, F              (4096 B)
.equ T2       0x9000        ; [T][D] int8 temp: O, relu(H)           (4096 B)
.equ S32      0xA000        ; [T][VPAD] int32 logits                 (2048 B)
.equ TMP32    0xB000        ; CHUNK int32 elementwise temp           (2048 B)
.equ S8       0xB800        ; [T][T] int8 scores                     (1024 B)
.equ P8       0xBC00        ; [T][T] int8 attention weights          (1024 B)
.equ KTP      0xC000        ; [T][D] trits — K as an MXU weight      (1024 B)
.equ VTP      0xC400        ; [D][T] trits — V^T as an MXU weight    (1024 B)
.equ MASK     0xC800        ; [T][T] int8 causal mask, 0 / -128      (1024 B)
.equ RQW      0xCC00        ; this layer's 16 requant {m0,n} words      (64 B)
.equ LOG      S32           ; the logits reuse the score-era buffer

; The two packed blocks are what the MXU reads as weight columns, and their
; layout is not a choice — it is the array's, reached by writing trits in the
; order they already lie in:
;
;   KTP  row t is K[t][:] packed, WCOL = 32 bytes. Column `s` of K^T is row `s`
;        of K, so a *row-major* pack is already column-major for the transpose,
;        and head h is the byte offset KHOFF*h into every row. That is why Q@K^T
;        needs no transpose at all.
;   VTP  row d is V^T[d][:] = V[:,d] packed, TCOL = 8 bytes. P@V contracts over
;        keys, which is V's *row* axis, so this one does need the transpose —
;        and the DMA can only transpose bytes, which is why V passes through
;        int8 (V8 -> D_VT -> VT) before it is ternarized.

; ---- the requant words, in the order adder_kernel.md §4 lists them ---------
; Reloaded from the layer block each iteration, so these addresses are fixed
; and every `setcfg scalar` stays a compile-time immediate. Four of the sixteen
; are consumed by an op other than `requant` — two `dyt` and two `tquant` — but
; the word format is identical, so DRAM staging does not care which.
.equ RQ_Q     RQW +  0      ; X @ Wq   -> Q      int8
.equ RQ_K     RQW +  4      ; X @ Wk   -> K      int8
.equ RQ_KT    RQW +  8      ; K        -> trits  `tquant`
.equ RQ_V     RQW + 12      ; X @ Wv   -> V      int8
.equ RQ_VT    RQW + 16      ; V^T      -> trits  `tquant`
.equ RQ_S     RQW + 20      ; Q@K^T int32 -> S8  (folds 1/sqrt(head_dim))
.equ RQ_ID    RQW + 24      ; {m0=1, n=0} — narrow + clip only
.equ RQ_P     RQW + 28      ; relu(S8) -> P8     ({1,0} is the natural choice)
.equ RQ_A     RQW + 32      ; P@V int32 -> A8
.equ RQ_O     RQW + 36      ; A8 @ Wo  -> O      **must land on X's scale**
.equ RQ_XO    RQW + 40      ; X + O    -> XO     **{1,0}: still on X's scale**
.equ RQ_X1    RQW + 44      ; XO + X   -> X1     `dyt`: carries norm1's alpha
.equ RQ_H     RQW + 48      ; X1 @ W1  -> H
.equ RQ_HR    RQW + 52      ; relu(H)  -> HR     ({1,0} is the natural choice)
.equ RQ_F     RQW + 56      ; HR @ W2  -> F      **must land on X1's scale**
.equ RQ_X2    RQW + 60      ; X1 + F   -> X2     `dyt`: carries norm2's alpha

; ===========================================================================
; DRAM map (512 KB available; highest byte used is 0x13FFF = 80 KB)
; ===========================================================================
; Globals live below 0x5000 so every one of them fits a 16-bit `li`. The layer
; blocks sit above and are reached through the `wl` base register instead.
.equ D_XIN    0x0000        ; [T][D] int8, the embedded input       (4096 B)
.equ D_MASK   0x1000        ; [T][T] int8 causal mask               (1024 B)
.equ D_A8     0x1400        ; [T][D] int8 attention out (scratch)   (4096 B)
.equ D_VT     0x2400        ; [D][T] int8 V^T (scratch)             (4096 B)
.equ D_WFC    0x3400        ; [D][VPAD] trits, fc.weight             (512 B)
.equ D_LOG    0x3C00        ; [T][VPAD] int32 logits — **the result** (2048 B)

.equ D_L0     0x5000        ; layer 0's block
.equ LSTRIDE  0x7400        ; per-layer block stride (29696 B)

; ---- offsets inside one layer block ---------------------------------------
.equ O_WQKV   0x0000        ; [D][3D] trits, col-major 2b          (12288 B)
.equ O_WO     0x3000        ; [D][D]  trits                         (4096 B)
.equ O_W1     0x4000        ; [D][F]  trits                         (4096 B)
.equ O_W2     0x5000        ; [F][D]  trits                         (4096 B)
.equ O_RQW    0x6000        ; this layer's 16 requant words            (64 B)
.equ O_XOUT   0x6400        ; [T][D] int8 — this layer's X, a checkpoint

; ---- byte counts ----------------------------------------------------------
.equ B_WQKV   D3 * WCOL     ; 12288
.equ B_WD     D * WCOL      ;  4096
.equ B_ACT    T * D         ;  4096   a [T][D] int8 activation
.equ B_VT     D * T         ;  4096
.equ B_SQ     T * T         ;  1024   a [T][T] int8 block
.equ B_TRIT   T * D * 2 // 8   ; 1024   a [T][D] tensor as packed trits
.equ B_RQW    64
.equ B_WFC    VPAD * WCOL   ;   512   the head is ternary: 32 bytes a column
.equ B_LOG    T * VPAD * 4  ;  2048

; ===========================================================================
; Registers — 30 of 31 are live.
; ===========================================================================
.reg sa    r1               ; scratchpad-side address scratch
.reg da    r2               ; DRAM-side address scratch
.reg rq    r3               ; address of the live requant {m0,n} word
.reg wbuf  r4
.reg x     r5
.reg vt    r6               ; VT: int8 V^T, the ternary pack's source
.reg s32   r7               ; the int32 logit block (also LOG)
.reg tmp   r8               ; TMP32
.reg s8a   r9
.reg s8b   r10
.reg p8a   r11
.reg p8b   r12
.reg mska  r13
.reg mskb  r14
.reg ah8   r15              ; head h's A column base inside T1
.reg q     r16              ; head h's Q column base
.reg kt    r17              ; head h's packed-K column base
.reg vth   r18              ; head h's packed-V^T column base
.reg i     r19              ; inner loop counter (heads, then chunks)
.reg n     r20              ; inner loop bound / stride scratch
; T1 and T2 have no permanent register: `pa`/`pb` carry them, re-`li`'d at each
; use, and double as the chunk-loop pointers.
.reg pa    r21
.reg pb    r22
.reg one   r23
.reg kchk  r24              ; CHUNK, the chunk pointer step
.reg kpck  r25              ; PKCH, the *packed* chunk pointer step (CHUNK/4)
.reg khd   r26              ; DH, the per-head int8 column step
.reg khk   r27              ; KHOFF, the per-head step into packed K
.reg khv   r28              ; VHOFF, the per-head step into packed V^T
.reg l     r29              ; layer counter
.reg wl    r30              ; this layer's DRAM block base

; ===========================================================================
; 0. Constants, fixed scratchpad bases, and the model-wide inputs
; ===========================================================================
    li      one,  1
    li      kchk, CHUNK
    li      kpck, PKCH
    li      khd,  DH
    li      khk,  KHOFF
    li      khv,  VHOFF

    li      wbuf, WBUF
    li      x,    X
    li      vt,   VT
    li      s32,  S32
    li      tmp,  TMP32
    li      s8a,  S8
    li      s8b,  S8 + CHUNK
    li      p8a,  P8
    li      p8b,  P8 + CHUNK
    li      mska, MASK
    li      mskb, MASK + CHUNK

    ; X and the mask are loaded once for the whole model. X then stays resident
    ; and every layer updates it in place — no inter-layer DRAM round trip.
    li      da, D_XIN
    setcfg  len, B_ACT
    rdmem   x, da
    li      da, D_MASK
    setcfg  len, B_SQ
    rdmem   mska, da

    ; The one MXU config that is the same for every matmul in the program. The
    ; rest (arow/wcol/crow/ktiles/ntiles) now differ between the D-deep
    ; projections and the two attention matmuls, so they are set per shape.
    setcfg  tlen, T             ; 32 token rows, one MXU pass (TOK_W = 6 bits)

    li      wl, D_L0
    li      l,  0

; ===========================================================================
; ============================ PER-LAYER BODY ===============================
; ===========================================================================
layer:
    ; ---- this layer's requant words, into the fixed RQW slot -------------
    ; Reloading them per layer is what keeps every `setcfg scalar` below a
    ; compile-time immediate instead of needing `setcfgr`.
    li      sa, O_RQW
    adds    da, wl, sa
    li      pa, RQW
    setcfg  len, B_RQW
    rdmem   pa, da

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. Q, K, V — three ternary matmuls out of one fused weight block
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; [Wq|Wk|Wv] is one [D][3D] block in DRAM, so it is one `rdmem`; but the three
; are *separate* matmuls because each carries its own absmean, hence its own
; requant word. Fusing them into a single 48-tile `matmul_t` would force one
; multiplier on all three (adder_kernel.md §4). Three 16-tile dispatches cost
; six extra instructions and keep each projection's scale its own.
;
; Unlike the previous revision the three land in three separate [T][D] buffers
; rather than interleaved into one [T][3D] block. `tquant` reads a contiguous
; vector, and K and V are both about to be ternarized, so a fused output would
; have to be de-interleaved first — the column stride that used to be free is
; now the thing in the way.

    adds    da, wl, r0          ; layer block base + O_WQKV (= 0)
    setcfg  len, B_WQKV
    rdmem   wbuf, da

    setcfg  arow,   AROW        ; activations are [T][D] row-major
    setcfg  wcol,   WCOL        ; weight columns are D trits = 32 bytes
    setcfg  ktiles, KTD         ; contract the full D = 16 array tiles
    setcfg  ntiles, NTD         ; 128 output columns per dispatch
    setcfg  crow,   CROWD       ; -> int8 rows of CROWD/4 = D bytes

    li      pa, Q8
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_Q
    matmul_t.rq  pa, x, wbuf                ; Q  [T][D] int8

    li      pa, K8
    li      pb, WBUF + D * WCOL
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_K
    matmul_t.rq  pa, x, pb                  ; K  [T][D] int8

    li      pa, V8
    li      pb, WBUF + 2 * D * WCOL
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_V
    matmul_t.rq  pa, x, pb                  ; V  [T][D] int8

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2. K -> trits, in place over the row axis
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `tquant` narrows int8 -> {-1,0,1} and writes the trit in the array's 2-bit
; weight encoding, four elements to a byte. Packed row-major, K's row t becomes
; the WCOL-byte weight column `t` of K^T — so the transpose in Q@K^T is free,
; exactly as it was when this was a vecmatmul, but now the array does the
; arithmetic. Eight CHUNK passes cover [T][D]; the destination advances PKCH.

    setcfg  vlen, CHUNK
    li      rq, RQ_KT
    li      pa, K8
    li      pb, KTP
    li      i,  0
    li      n,  NCHUNK
packk:
    tquant  pb, pa, rq
    adds    pa, pa, kchk
    adds    pb, pb, kpck
    adds    i,  i,  one
    cmps    i,  n
    blt     packk

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 3. V^T — every head, in one transposing DMA — then V^T -> trits
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; P @ V contracts over *keys*, which is V's row axis, so the array's weight
; column is a column of V. The `.t` DMA is the only thing here that can reorder
; bytes, and it moves bytes, not trits — which is why V is ternarized *after*
; the transpose and not before. Head h's rows land at VT + h*DH*T, so the packed
; per-head base is the plain pointer step VHOFF.

    li      pa, V8
    li      da, D_VT
    setcfg  tcols, D            ; D elements per source row...
    setcfg  tsrow, AROW         ; ...from rows D bytes apart
    setcfg  tdrow, T            ; destination rows are T tokens long
    setcfg  len,   B_VT
    wrmem.t pa, da              ; scratchpad V -> DRAM V^T
    rdmem   vt, da              ; plain fill back (the mode is in the instr)

    setcfg  vlen, CHUNK
    li      rq, RQ_VT
    li      pa, VT
    li      pb, VTP
    li      i,  0
    li      n,  NCHUNK
packv:
    tquant  pb, pa, rq
    adds    pa, pa, kchk
    adds    pb, pb, kpck
    adds    i,  i,  one
    cmps    i,  n
    blt     packv

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 4. Attention, one head at a time — both matmuls on the MXU
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Head h is a contiguous DH-column slice of Q (kv_heads == q_heads, so
; heads_per_q = 1 and the model's 5-D layout collapses to [T, 4, 32]) and a
; byte offset into each packed block. No reshape anywhere.

    li      q,   Q8                         ; head 0's Q columns
    li      kt,  KTP                        ; head 0's packed K columns
    li      vth, VTP                        ; head 0's packed V^T rows
    li      ah8, T1                         ; head 0's A column block
    li      i,   0
    li      n,   HEADS

head:
    ; ---- S8 = requant(Q_h @ K_h^T) --------------------------------------
    ; K^T is never materialized: the packed weight column `s` *is* K's row `s`.
    ; The requant is the array's own store-side one, so RQ_S (which carries
    ; 1/sqrt(head_dim)) costs no separate pass — the two VPU requants the old
    ; vecmatmul needed to narrow its int32 block are gone with it.
    setcfg  arow,   AROW        ; Q rows are D bytes apart
    setcfg  wcol,   WCOL        ; a packed K row is D trits = 32 bytes
    setcfg  crow,   SROW        ; -> int8 rows of SROW/4 = T bytes
    setcfg  ktiles, KTDH        ; contract over the head dim
    setcfg  ntiles, NTT         ; T keys out
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_S
    matmul_t.rq  s8a, q, kt     ; S8[t][s] int8

    ; ---- P = relu(S8 + mask), in two CHUNK passes ------------------------
    ; The mask is *data*, not control flow: an int8 tensor of 0 (allowed) and
    ; -128 (masked). vecadd widens to int32, the {1,0} requant narrows back and
    ; clips, so a masked entry lands in [-128,-1] — always negative, therefore
    ; always zero after relu — while an allowed entry adds 0 and is unchanged.
    ; That is exactly the model's `relu(scores + -1e9)`, and it is exact for
    ; every score scale rather than a tolerance.
    setcfg  vlen, CHUNK
    li      rq, RQ_ID
    vecadd  tmp, s8a, mska      ; + causal mask   -> int32
    requant s8a, tmp, rq        ; -> int8, masked entries now <= -1
    vecadd  tmp, s8b, mskb
    requant s8b, tmp, rq
    li      rq, RQ_P
    relu    tmp, s8a            ; masked -> 0     -> int32
    requant p8a, tmp, rq        ; -> int8 attention weights
    relu    tmp, s8b
    requant p8b, tmp, rq

    ; ---- A[:, h*DH : (h+1)*DH] = requant(P_h @ V_h) ---------------------
    ; The output tile lands *directly* in A's column block: `crow` = CROWD makes
    ; the requantized store step D bytes per token row, and the destination base
    ; carries the head's column offset. The previous revision computed A^T and
    ; scattered it back with a `.t` spill through DRAM; that whole detour, and
    ; the DRAM round trip that followed it, existed only because `vecmatmul`
    ; could not write a strided sub-block.
    setcfg  arow,   T           ; P rows are T keys
    setcfg  wcol,   TCOL        ; a packed V^T row is T trits = 8 bytes
    setcfg  crow,   CROWD       ; -> int8 rows of CROWD/4 = D bytes
    setcfg  ktiles, KTT         ; contract over keys
    setcfg  ntiles, NTDH        ; DH columns out
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_A
    matmul_t.rq  ah8, p8a, vth  ; A[t][h*DH + j] int8

    ; ---- next head ------------------------------------------------------
    adds    q,   q,   khd       ; Q advances one head_dim of int8 columns
    adds    kt,  kt,  khk       ; ...packed K, one head_dim of trits
    adds    vth, vth, khv       ; ...packed V^T, DH whole rows
    adds    ah8, ah8, khd
    adds    i,   i,   one
    cmps    i,   n
    blt     head

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 5. O = A @ Wo, then the attention residual X <- norm1(X + (O + X))
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A is already assembled in T1, so the only thing that moves it is the
; verification checkpoint below — the Wo matmul reads it where it lies.

    li      pa, T1
    li      da, D_A8
    setcfg  len, B_ACT
    wrmem   pa, da              ; checkpoint A (adder_kernel.md §7), not a path

    li      sa, O_WO
    adds    da, wl, sa
    setcfg  len, B_WD
    rdmem   wbuf, da

    setcfg  arow,   AROW        ; back to the D-deep projection geometry
    setcfg  wcol,   WCOL
    setcfg  ktiles, KTD
    setcfg  ntiles, NTD
    setcfg  crow,   CROWD
    li      pa, T1
    li      pb, T2
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_O
    matmul_t.rq  pb, pa, wbuf   ; O = requant(A @ Wo)   [T][D] int8

    ; The attention residual is `2X + O`, not `X + O`: MultiHeadAttention
    ; returns `O + X` and Transformer adds `X` again. `vecadd` takes two int8
    ; operands, so that is two passes with a narrow between them.
    ;
    ; XO = requant(X + O), written *over* O. Both operands are int8 at the same
    ; scale (that is RQ_O's job) and RQ_XO is {1,0}, so XO stays on X's scale and
    ; is a legal operand for the second add. Overwriting O is safe because the
    ; vecadd has already consumed the whole chunk — dispatches are atomic.
    setcfg  vlen, CHUNK
    li      rq, RQ_XO
    li      pa, X
    li      pb, T2
    li      i,  0
    li      n,  NCHUNK
attnxo:
    vecadd  tmp, pa, pb
    requant pb,  tmp, rq
    adds    pa,  pa, kchk
    adds    pb,  pb, kchk
    adds    i,   i,  one
    cmps    i,   n
    blt     attnxo

    ; X1 = norm1(XO + X) = DyT(...), in place over X. `dyt` is the same narrow
    ; as `requant` with a symmetric clip: RQ_X1's multiplier carries norm1's
    ; alpha and lands the result on 1/127, at which point saturating at +-127 is
    ; exactly hardtanh saturating at +-1. No extra pass — the residual add had
    ; to be narrowed anyway, so the normalization is free.
    li      rq, RQ_X1
    li      pa, X
    li      pb, T2
    li      i,  0
    li      n,  NCHUNK
resid1:
    vecadd  tmp, pa, pb
    dyt     pa,  tmp, rq
    adds    pa,  pa, kchk
    adds    pb,  pb, kchk
    adds    i,   i,  one
    cmps    i,   n
    blt     resid1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 6. Feed-forward: X <- norm2(X1 + W2(relu(W1(X1))))
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    li      sa, O_W1
    adds    da, wl, sa
    setcfg  len, B_WD
    rdmem   wbuf, da
    li      pa, T1
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_H
    matmul_t.rq  pa, x, wbuf    ; H = requant(X1 @ W1)   [T][F] int8

    setcfg  vlen, CHUNK
    li      rq, RQ_HR
    li      pa, T1
    li      pb, T2
    li      i,  0
    li      n,  NCHUNK
ffrelu:
    relu    tmp, pa             ; int8 -> int32
    requant pb,  tmp, rq        ; -> int8
    adds    pa,  pa, kchk
    adds    pb,  pb, kchk
    adds    i,   i,  one
    cmps    i,   n
    blt     ffrelu

    li      sa, O_W2
    adds    da, wl, sa
    setcfg  len, B_WD
    rdmem   wbuf, da
    li      pa, T1
    li      pb, T2
    setcfg  scalar, RQ_F
    matmul_t.rq  pa, pb, wbuf   ; F = requant(relu(H) @ W2)   [T][D] int8

    ; X2 = norm2(X1 + F), again in place and again requiring RQ_F to have landed
    ; F on X1's scale. RQ_X2 carries norm2's alpha and targets 1/127, so this
    ; `dyt` is what makes the *next* layer's input scale known analytically
    ; rather than calibrated — see sharp edge 3 at the top.
    setcfg  vlen, CHUNK
    li      rq, RQ_X2
    li      pa, X
    li      pb, T1
    li      i,  0
    li      n,  NCHUNK
resid2:
    vecadd  tmp, pa, pb
    dyt     pa,  tmp, rq
    adds    pa,  pa, kchk
    adds    pb,  pb, kchk
    adds    i,   i,  one
    cmps    i,   n
    blt     resid2

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 7. Checkpoint this layer's X, then advance to the next layer block
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; X stays resident in the scratchpad; this spill exists so every layer boundary
; is a comparable intermediate for the integer reference (adder_kernel.md §7),
; not because the data has to move.
    li      sa, O_XOUT
    adds    da, wl, sa
    setcfg  len, B_ACT
    wrmem   x, da

    li      n,  LSTRIDE         ; `n` is free here — the inner loops are done
    adds    wl, wl, n
    adds    l,  l,  one
    li      n,  LAYERS
    cmps    l,  n
    blt     layer

; ===========================================================================
; 8. Output head: logits = X @ fc.weight
; ===========================================================================
; `Model.fc` is a TernaryLinear now, so this is an ordinary `matmul_t` against
; the same D-deep geometry the projections use — the array multiplies an int8
; activation by a ternary weight, and X arrives from norm2 at 1/127 as exactly
; that. It was the last `vecmatmul` in the program (int8 X against an int8
; fc.weight, which only the VPU can do); the VPU now runs no matmul at all.
;
; `TernaryLinear.w` is [in, out] = [D][VOCAB] already, which is the column-major
; orientation the array packs a weight in, so nothing is transposed — the same
; property that made `nn.Linear`'s [out, in] convenient for `vecmatmul`, one
; layout over.
;
; **VPAD, not VOCAB.** The array writes a whole COLS-wide tile at a time, so a
; 13-column output is two tiles and the second one writes columns 8..15. With a
; 13-word row stride, columns 13..15 of token t would land on columns 0..2 of
; token t+1 — and `n` is the *outer* loop, so the second tile overwrites the
; first tile's work for every row but the last. Padding the head to 16 columns
; costs 3 zero weight columns (96 bytes) and 384 bytes of logit block, and makes
; the row stride a whole tile. The host stages zero trits above VOCAB and reads
; 13 of every 16 words back.
;
; Spilled as raw int32 with no requant: the host only needs an argmax over 13
; logits, and narrowing first would throw away resolution for nothing.

    li      da, D_WFC
    setcfg  len, B_WFC
    rdmem   wbuf, da

    setcfg  arow,   AROW        ; X is [T][D] row-major, as ever
    setcfg  wcol,   WCOL        ; a head column is D trits = 32 bytes
    setcfg  ktiles, KTD         ; contract the whole model dim
    setcfg  ntiles, NTV         ; VPAD columns out, two tiles
    setcfg  crow,   LROW        ; int32 rows of VPAD words
    matmul_t s32, x, wbuf       ; LOG[t][v] = sum_d X[t][d] * W[d][v]

    li      da, D_LOG
    setcfg  len, B_LOG
    wrmem   s32, da
    halt
